GPUs have emerged as foundational platforms for high-performance computing and machine learning. However, rapid architectural evolution challenges existing performance evaluations. Cycle-accurate simulation offers high precision but is prohibitively slow for large-scale design exploration. Sampling-based acceleration techniques struggle with behavior characterization, stable-state detection, and execution time prediction, thus constraining their applicability in complex GPU workloads. To address these limitations, we propose GRASP, a fine-grained and adaptive sampled simulation framework for GPU workloads that incorporates three principal innovations: (1) a semantic-aware kernel representation utilizing a BERT-based instruction embedding technique to replace the traditional Basic Block Vector (BBV), enabling a more effective description of GPU kernel behavior; (2) a state vector-driven stability detection mechanism that identifies program stability by dynamically comparing global and local state vectors to avoid local optimization pitfalls; and (3) a few-shot execution time prediction model employing a lightweight decision tree regression approach to capture the diverse execution characteristics of various basic blocks. Extensive experiments on both single-kernel and multi-kernel benchmarks demonstrate that GRASP outperforms Photon, the current state-of-the-art framework: in single-kernel scenarios, it reduces the average error from 7.19% to 5.52% while increasing the speedup from 2.46× to 5.59×; in multi-kernel cases, the average error is reduced from 20.67% to 13.01%, without compromising speedup.